  "question": {
    "text": "<p>A square loop of side <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> is placed inside a long solenoid that has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>50</mn></math> turns per centimetre and carries a sinusoidally varying current of amplitude <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> and angular frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>700</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>rad</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>. The central axes of the loop and solenoid coincide. The amplitude of the emf induced in the loop is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math> is ___________</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo></math> Take, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced open=\"\"><mrow><mi>&#960;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>22</mn><mn>7</mn></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math></p>"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "44",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p><img src=\"assets\\st0033_4c4401f16dfd.png\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 174px;\" /></p><p>It is given that the current is varying sinusoidally. The current can be written as&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>sin</mi><mi>&#969;</mi><mi>t</mi></math>.</p><p>The magnetic field through the solenoid&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>n</mi><mi>I</mi></math></p><p>The flux through the square is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#981;</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>n</mi><mi>I</mi><mi>A</mi></math></p><p>The emf is</p><p>&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#949;</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>n</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>sin</mi><mi>&#969;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>&#949;</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>n</mi><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>&#969;</mi><mi>cos</mi><mi>&#969;</mi><mi>t</mi></math></p><p>The amplitude of the emf is,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#949;</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>n</mi><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>&#969;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><mn>50</mn><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>700</mn><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>&#949;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>44</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math></p></blockquote>"
  }
}